From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jim Martin <jimmartin@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 13:54:23 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] drm/msm: Compress external DisplayPort streams with DSC

The DP controller only drives uncompressed streams, so a mode that
needs more bandwidth than the link carries, or one wider than the two
layer mixers that feed a DP interface (5120 pixels), is rejected. Large
external monitors therefore run below their native mode.

Add Display Stream Compression with forward error correction for
external DisplayPort sinks that decode both (DPCD 1.4, DSC 1.1 or 1.2,
8 bpc RGB), on the DP controllers whose descriptor enables it. Only
Glymur's do; other SoCs offer the same modes as before. In the DP
driver:

- read the sink's FEC and DSC capabilities with the other DPCD caps;
- in mode validation, try an uncompressed fit first and otherwise build
  a DSC configuration (8 bpc RGB compressed to 8 bpp, slice count by
  pixel rate and source topology) and check the compressed rate,
  including FEC overhead, against the link. Modes wider than 5120
  pixels are accepted only compressed;
- program the data throttle, the compression control and the PPS
  secondary data packet, sent with every frame through the peripheral
  flush; SDP payload parity is computed over four bytes;
- tell the sink FEC_READY before training, start FEC once the link
  carries video (up to three attempts, as the FEC sequence can collide
  with blanking-start symbols), then enable decompression in the sink;
  undo both when the stream stops;
- export the DSC configuration and the per-mode decision to the DPU.

In the DPU:

- ask the DP driver whether the mode being checked will be compressed,
  because DP decides compression per mode and only programs it in the
  bridge pre-enable, after the DPU topology has been chosen;
- return the DP stream's DSC configuration from
  dpu_encoder_get_dsc_config(), and get the extra interface timing the DP
  driver derives for it (pixels per interface clock, extra data
  throttle cycles and active width) through
  dpu_encoder_get_dp_dsc_timing();
- program the DP interface for a compressed stream: divide the
  horizontal timing by the DP pixel clock factor, add the extra width
  to the active area and back porch, set the compressed data width and
  INTF_CFG2_DCE_DATA_COMPRESS, and take the data window from the data
  throttle count;
- refresh the PPS and request the peripheral flush on every frame of a
  compressed DP stream, as Qualcomm's downstream driver does;
- explicitly clear the endian flip of the ping-pong DCE output on DPU 7
  and later when the block is bound to a DSC encoder, instead of relying
  on the value left in the register.

The slice table and the data-throttle calculation follow Qualcomm's
downstream DisplayPort driver.

The DPU programs uncompressed DP and eDP streams as before; the
ping-pong byte swap is also cleared for DSI DSC on DPU 7 and later. In
the DP driver, behaviour for uncompressed streams changes slightly:
training now clears FEC in the main link control, writes
FEC_CONFIGURATION only when a compressed stream needs it or the previous
one set it, and the link budget in mode validation
uses the full pixel clock also for YUV 4:2:0-only modes, so a few of
those that fit may be rejected.

Experimental; exercised only on the Surface Laptop 8 (Glymur).

Assisted-by: Opus 5.5
Signed-off-by: Jim Martin <jimmartin@gmail.com>
